    {
      "abstract": "In this study, a hybrid integral-equation method (HIEM) for the calculation of seaquake load is proposed. The seaquake potential satisfying the three-dimensional (3D) Helmholtz equation is solved by coupling a set of boundary integral equations for the interior domain and eigenfunction expansions for the exterior domain with matching boundary conditions. Verification studies are subsequently conducted based on free-field analysis and a diffraction load calculation. Characteristics of the seaquake loading are systematically investigated with respect to a 15-MW semi-submersible floating wind turbine. The effects of acoustic-gravity waves, compressibility, water depth, wave radiation, and wave diffraction are checked. Two selected past earthquake records, El Centro (1940) and Taft (1952), are upscaled using a spectral matching method targeting a leased area in the Californian sea per API RP2EQ code. Subsequently, seaquake-load time series are generated, which is then combined with 3-axis seabed earthquakes applied to anchor positions in a mid-fidelity model for aero-servo-hydro-elastic-mooring coupled simulations. For this case study, seaquake loads affect the platform motion, tower-base bending moment, and nacelle accelerations more significantly than movement of seabed alone. Conversely, mooring line tensions are more influenced by earthquakes than by seaquake loads.",
